WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.530 --> 00:00:05.530
AMAR RAOL: Welcome on
the Google Cloud Next.

00:00:05.530 --> 00:00:07.180
It's a pleasure to see you all.

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:09.320
Hope you guys had a
really good morning.

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:11.896
Was that exciting, the keynotes?

00:00:11.896 --> 00:00:13.210
Yeah?

00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:14.650
The Googlers were
not allowed in,

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:18.500
so I have to rely on
your reaction here.

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:20.000
Thank you for coming
to our session.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:21.160
My name is Amar Raol.

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:23.800
I'm from our professional
services organization

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:26.020
here in Google Cloud.

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:28.330
It's our team's
responsibility to help

00:00:28.330 --> 00:00:30.280
our customers come
onto our platform

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:32.380
as successfully as possible.

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:35.050
So I'm joined today by
Heather Traher, who's

00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:38.410
from our user research team,
and also Flint Waters, also

00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:40.970
on our professional
services organization.

00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:44.470
And what we want to take you
through in this next hour

00:00:44.470 --> 00:00:46.600
is a typical journey
that our customers

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:49.840
go through when they're
moving from a legacy

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:53.650
environment for messaging and
collaboration onto G Suite.

00:00:53.650 --> 00:00:57.790
So I wanted to give you a
very in-depth overview of what

00:00:57.790 --> 00:01:00.580
that journey looks like, how
we actually bring customers

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:03.400
in successfully, what are
the strategies and the tools

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:07.420
that we and our partners
actually employ when we're

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:10.039
working with our customers,
and try and give you

00:01:10.039 --> 00:01:11.830
some comfort on how to
make that transition

00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:17.401
happen if you yourselves were
considering a move to G Suite.

00:01:17.401 --> 00:01:19.150
Heather will actually
take you through how

00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:22.410
we engage with the long-term,
once you're onto our platform,

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:24.760
and Flint will kind
of give a eyewitness

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:26.800
account of his
transition when he

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:30.670
was the CEO of the
State of Wyoming.

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:32.090
So let's get started.

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:37.010
I think one of the key things
I want you to understand

00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:40.100
is we've been doing this
for a very long time.

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:44.090
I've been with Google now for
over five and a half years,

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:47.360
deploying customers onto
our platforms for both G

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:49.010
Suite and GCP.

00:01:49.010 --> 00:01:53.074
These are exclusively
customers who are on G Suite.

00:01:53.074 --> 00:01:55.490
Now they're certainly evaluating
other parts of our Google

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:58.610
Cloud, but you can recognize
some of these logos.

00:01:58.610 --> 00:02:04.760
These are very large, complex,
multi-national organizations.

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:06.900
Not a single one of them
is a digital native.

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:11.000
Right, it's not just a
Snapchat or a Spotify.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:13.180
These are traditional
customers who

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:15.650
were looking for transformation
in their business

00:02:15.650 --> 00:02:17.780
and how they collaborated
with each other,

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:22.190
how their staff worked with each
other, and what sorts of tools

00:02:22.190 --> 00:02:26.820
they can enable their staff
with in their move to the cloud.

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:31.410
So I want to give that idea
that this is no longer a domain.

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:36.050
G Suite it is no longer a domain
for small companies, SMBs.

00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:37.910
We work with very
large organizations

00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:40.810
in that transition and
certainly, you can do this too.

00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:43.580
So let me take you through what
a typical journey would look

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:46.700
like and what are the strategies
we employ to help customers

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:49.580
like this move to our platform.

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:54.050
So our whole approach
rests on two main concepts.

00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:56.300
That it's not just
an IT project and we

00:02:56.300 --> 00:02:59.390
want to be with you
for the long period.

00:02:59.390 --> 00:03:02.030
You can't change mindsets
in a short amount of time

00:03:02.030 --> 00:03:04.700
and we want to be a partner
with you for the long haul.

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:07.470
So let's dive into these two
specific concepts and that

00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:10.950
will make up our methodology
when it comes together.

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:14.900
So first, when we actually
deploy the G Suite

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:17.600
set of tools with
a large customer,

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:20.529
we go with the tried and true
process of people, process,

00:03:20.529 --> 00:03:22.070
and technology, and
make sure that we

00:03:22.070 --> 00:03:24.330
address each of those concepts.

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:27.710
So the technology bit is
the technical components

00:03:27.710 --> 00:03:32.270
to actually flip the
switches into bringing

00:03:32.270 --> 00:03:34.730
your staff into the cloud.

00:03:34.730 --> 00:03:36.500
That could be the
messaging environment.

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:39.860
So if you start now, you go from
a legacy messaging environment

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:44.930
to using Gmail as your primary
corporate email system,

00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:46.770
and also our
collaboration tools.

00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:49.610
So using Docs, and
Drive, and Hangouts.

00:03:49.610 --> 00:03:52.910
So I'll cover that in
a very specific way

00:03:52.910 --> 00:03:55.810
and how we actually do
the technical rollout.

00:03:55.810 --> 00:03:58.757
The people component of this
is the changed management.

00:03:58.757 --> 00:04:01.340
This is how do you make people
aware of the change that you're

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:03.860
about to deploy, how do
you communicate, how do you

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:05.960
train them and the
options and the strategies

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:06.890
that you use there.

00:04:06.890 --> 00:04:09.644
So I'll go into that in a
little bit of detail as well.

00:04:09.644 --> 00:04:11.060
But the important
piece that we've

00:04:11.060 --> 00:04:13.670
evolved our methodology
into is that third bucket,

00:04:13.670 --> 00:04:15.140
which is the process.

00:04:15.140 --> 00:04:18.110
We didn't just want to
deploy the technical tools

00:04:18.110 --> 00:04:19.610
and then cut and run.

00:04:19.610 --> 00:04:22.749
We wanted to work with
our staff at organizations

00:04:22.749 --> 00:04:24.290
and make sure that
they were applying

00:04:24.290 --> 00:04:27.350
those tools effectively in their
everyday business processes.

00:04:27.350 --> 00:04:29.144
That's a very important
concept for us

00:04:29.144 --> 00:04:30.560
and we have a
number of strategies

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:34.870
to go deeper into that, so
I'll talk to that as well.

00:04:34.870 --> 00:04:37.030
And together, we feel that
if you address all three

00:04:37.030 --> 00:04:38.920
of these components,
the organization that's

00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:40.419
looking for that
true transformation

00:04:40.419 --> 00:04:42.970
can really reach those goals.

00:04:42.970 --> 00:04:46.600
That second concept was
around the long-term.

00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:50.350
Our methodology does not stop
at the technical deployment.

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:52.390
We want to work with
you for the long haul.

00:04:52.390 --> 00:04:54.430
So in that, our
approach actually

00:04:54.430 --> 00:04:57.250
includes things how do you
measure how successful you

00:04:57.250 --> 00:04:58.960
are in Google?

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:01.150
What sort of impact
are you having?

00:05:01.150 --> 00:05:03.310
What sort of access
can we give you

00:05:03.310 --> 00:05:07.270
to our road maps and our
future vision for the tools,

00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:10.420
and how can you apply them
back into your organization?

00:05:10.420 --> 00:05:12.612
So we'll talk a little
bit about that as well.

00:05:12.612 --> 00:05:15.070
So when you bring these two
concepts together, you in fact,

00:05:15.070 --> 00:05:17.440
get what our
methodology actually

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:20.864
is for deploying G Suite at
medium to large customers.

00:05:20.864 --> 00:05:22.780
Certainly, all of these
concepts can be scaled

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:25.400
to small businesses as well.

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:27.190
But this is a deep methodology.

00:05:27.190 --> 00:05:29.050
There's lots of detail
behind it and I'll

00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:31.984
take it through just some key
concepts in each of these.

00:05:31.984 --> 00:05:34.150
I'll focus on the middle
three, the people, process,

00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:37.204
and the technology, and we'll
go through that very quickly.

00:05:37.204 --> 00:05:38.370
So the technology component.

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:41.520
I'll work my way up.

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:45.090
The toolset is a deep platform.

00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:47.550
It's a very important
concept that if you

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:49.530
are evaluating a
move to G Suite,

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:53.070
it is not just a messaging move.

00:05:53.070 --> 00:05:55.050
We're not just talking
about mail and calendar.

00:05:55.050 --> 00:05:57.750
We're talking about a number of
tools that are available to you

00:05:57.750 --> 00:06:00.027
now once you come
onto that platform,

00:06:00.027 --> 00:06:01.860
and it's an important
concept to think about

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:05.130
because there's lots of
opportunities around the legacy

00:06:05.130 --> 00:06:07.860
environments that you can
displace if you were to come

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:09.654
onto a platform this rich.

00:06:09.654 --> 00:06:11.070
So you see on the
left there, when

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:13.550
we work with our customers,
we're be talking about not only

00:06:13.550 --> 00:06:17.280
mail and calendar, but
how they manage contacts,

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:20.610
how they get access through
mobile, the Docs, and Drive,

00:06:20.610 --> 00:06:22.350
and the collaboration suite.

00:06:22.350 --> 00:06:25.020
These are the productivity
tools that are made available.

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:27.180
The communication
tools that are also

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:29.280
available through Hangouts.

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:33.430
The method by which you
access using Chrome and so on.

00:06:33.430 --> 00:06:34.780
So those are also there.

00:06:34.780 --> 00:06:39.480
The platform also includes
our e-discovery toolset

00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:42.180
as well, which is Vault.
So if you have environments

00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:46.140
right now that need to do
discovery and compliance,

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:49.650
you can achieve those goals
using the G Suite platform.

00:06:49.650 --> 00:06:52.680
We also have options around
data loss prevention,

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:55.110
how you actually authenticate
with the toolsets.

00:06:55.110 --> 00:06:58.530
So there's lots of detail
there that you can evaluate on

00:06:58.530 --> 00:07:00.780
whether you want to use
Google's authentication,

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:04.290
or if you want to use your
own SSO, or a third party.

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:06.300
Those are there if you
want to use two factor

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:08.610
authentication or keycards.

00:07:08.610 --> 00:07:11.940
These things are made available
to you using the platform.

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:13.685
So it's a very flexible
platform and we

00:07:13.685 --> 00:07:16.530
make sure that its
clear to the team

00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:20.760
that this is a fully
cloud environment.

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:21.870
Right?

00:07:21.870 --> 00:07:27.440
Its much easier to deploy,
maintain, and manage.

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:30.540
All of these tools here, in
fact, all work in the browser.

00:07:30.540 --> 00:07:32.580
There's nothing
for you to install

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:35.040
except for that browser.

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:39.420
Now, we've supported all the
major modern browsers here

00:07:39.420 --> 00:07:40.170
with our toolset.

00:07:43.790 --> 00:07:47.360
Also, if you think about
the concept of the data

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:50.360
that you're generating when
we're talking about messaging

00:07:50.360 --> 00:07:54.170
and productivity, when all
of that data is in the cloud,

00:07:54.170 --> 00:07:57.590
you actually have a more
comprehensive data management

00:07:57.590 --> 00:07:58.960
solution as well.

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:02.810
So when you need to do
compliance all your data is--

00:08:02.810 --> 00:08:04.850
you're actually doing
compliance on the data

00:08:04.850 --> 00:08:08.000
where it's being created
and where it's being stored.

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:11.060
You don't have to shimmy things
around in multiple environments

00:08:11.060 --> 00:08:15.320
to create some of that
logic for your policies.

00:08:15.320 --> 00:08:17.160
So that's an important
concept as well.

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:18.890
So think about the
G Suite environment

00:08:18.890 --> 00:08:21.170
as a full suite of
tools that go well

00:08:21.170 --> 00:08:23.120
beyond just mail and calendar.

00:08:23.120 --> 00:08:24.572
OK?

00:08:24.572 --> 00:08:26.030
The other thing is
that when you're

00:08:26.030 --> 00:08:28.310
coming onto our
environment, we want

00:08:28.310 --> 00:08:32.360
to make sure that you're
aware of all the things

00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:34.070
that our teams will
actually take it

00:08:34.070 --> 00:08:36.559
through for that migration.

00:08:36.559 --> 00:08:40.580
These are the 10 topics that we
will cover during a migration

00:08:40.580 --> 00:08:41.720
to G Suite.

00:08:41.720 --> 00:08:43.700
Our partners and Google
will work with you

00:08:43.700 --> 00:08:48.770
on each of these topics, and if
you're a medium sized business

00:08:48.770 --> 00:08:51.230
or a large, complex
organization like the logos

00:08:51.230 --> 00:08:53.960
that I spoke through,
these are essentially,

00:08:53.960 --> 00:08:56.870
the technical work streams
that we will go very deeply

00:08:56.870 --> 00:08:59.610
into as we do this migration.

00:08:59.610 --> 00:09:01.940
So the point here is that
you should feel reassured

00:09:01.940 --> 00:09:04.010
that yes, you will
have applications

00:09:04.010 --> 00:09:06.681
in your current environment
that rely on mail.

00:09:06.681 --> 00:09:08.930
We will talk through those
through an integration work

00:09:08.930 --> 00:09:09.770
stream.

00:09:09.770 --> 00:09:11.270
You'll want to know
OK, how do I get

00:09:11.270 --> 00:09:13.830
to these systems
with mobile devices?

00:09:13.830 --> 00:09:16.320
How do I set up my mobile
fleet in my environment?

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:18.800
We'll talk through that
in the mobile work stream.

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:21.740
How do I set up the policies,
and make the lawyers happy,

00:09:21.740 --> 00:09:23.950
and address compliance issues?

00:09:23.950 --> 00:09:26.510
We'll talk through that
in the Vault work stream.

00:09:26.510 --> 00:09:28.430
We'll set those policies up.

00:09:28.430 --> 00:09:32.360
So if we can tackle
these 10 items with you,

00:09:32.360 --> 00:09:35.894
this is a successful transition
to the cloud with G Suite.

00:09:38.632 --> 00:09:40.840
I want to zoom in a little
bit now that we're talking

00:09:40.840 --> 00:09:42.250
about the technology component.

00:09:42.250 --> 00:09:44.710
I want to zoom in on
the middle piece here,

00:09:44.710 --> 00:09:46.960
which is the enablement piece.

00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:50.530
This is the part where we
actually do the technical move

00:09:50.530 --> 00:09:52.330
to G Suite.

00:09:52.330 --> 00:09:56.410
We do it typically in three
steps, core IT, early adopters,

00:09:56.410 --> 00:09:58.200
and global go live.

00:09:58.200 --> 00:10:01.090
And these three steps
are very methodical,

00:10:01.090 --> 00:10:03.970
especially when it comes
to the messaging component.

00:10:03.970 --> 00:10:08.575
So what I mean by that is in
the core IT phase of a typical G

00:10:08.575 --> 00:10:12.490
Suite deployment,
its very front loaded

00:10:12.490 --> 00:10:15.250
in terms of the technical
work that needs to happen.

00:10:15.250 --> 00:10:17.800
We set up our
authentication process.

00:10:17.800 --> 00:10:20.560
We set up how we are
provisioning users.

00:10:20.560 --> 00:10:24.810
We set up our strategy for
deploying the tools to mobile.

00:10:24.810 --> 00:10:27.070
We'll also bring a
small fleet of users

00:10:27.070 --> 00:10:28.990
into the platform as
quickly as possible,

00:10:28.990 --> 00:10:32.140
without really affecting your
current environment, typically

00:10:32.140 --> 00:10:35.520
just forwarding mail
over to G Suite.

00:10:35.520 --> 00:10:36.970
But when we get
to early adopters,

00:10:36.970 --> 00:10:39.370
this is our practice run.

00:10:39.370 --> 00:10:43.690
We actually bring in 5%
to 10% of the organization

00:10:43.690 --> 00:10:48.140
to become advocates for this
change that you're engaging in.

00:10:48.140 --> 00:10:50.830
And we call them early adopters.

00:10:50.830 --> 00:10:52.900
When these folks
come on, we actually

00:10:52.900 --> 00:10:55.300
test the technical
environment that we set up

00:10:55.300 --> 00:10:58.210
during that core IT phase.

00:10:58.210 --> 00:11:00.040
It's the early adopter
phase that we also

00:11:00.040 --> 00:11:03.070
switch the MX record, if
you are doing a messaging

00:11:03.070 --> 00:11:05.500
rollout, to Google directly.

00:11:05.500 --> 00:11:07.750
That means all your mail now
starts flowing to Google,

00:11:07.750 --> 00:11:10.790
and those folks who remain
in Google, those 5% to 10%,

00:11:10.790 --> 00:11:12.970
the mail will stay there,
and the rest of the folks,

00:11:12.970 --> 00:11:16.540
they will get forwarded back
to your legacy environment.

00:11:16.540 --> 00:11:18.130
So it's a step process.

00:11:18.130 --> 00:11:22.260
It's a phased deployment
into our environment.

00:11:22.260 --> 00:11:27.070
What's important about this
early adopters group as well,

00:11:27.070 --> 00:11:29.500
is that it tests
out your training

00:11:29.500 --> 00:11:31.780
and your communications
plans as well.

00:11:31.780 --> 00:11:34.330
This is that dry run for when
the rest of the organization

00:11:34.330 --> 00:11:35.500
comes in.

00:11:35.500 --> 00:11:37.340
You're also building
advocates that

00:11:37.340 --> 00:11:39.704
help your supported
environment scale.

00:11:39.704 --> 00:11:41.620
Right, so when the rest
of the group comes in,

00:11:41.620 --> 00:11:45.070
you'll have somebody in the
office within arm's reach

00:11:45.070 --> 00:11:48.070
that you can ask questions to.

00:11:48.070 --> 00:11:49.610
Finally, we have
a global go live.

00:11:49.610 --> 00:11:52.390
This is where the majority
of the organization

00:11:52.390 --> 00:11:54.130
will come in at the same time.

00:11:54.130 --> 00:11:59.830
We try to minimize ideas
around phase go lives.

00:11:59.830 --> 00:12:02.770
So this is contrary to
typical IT deployments

00:12:02.770 --> 00:12:06.610
where we do multiple,
multiple phases.

00:12:06.610 --> 00:12:09.700
In fact, we try and limit
it to these three phases,

00:12:09.700 --> 00:12:12.070
if not four, and the
reason for that is we

00:12:12.070 --> 00:12:14.830
want to bring users into
the G Suite environment who

00:12:14.830 --> 00:12:20.150
work together, to minimize
something called coexistence.

00:12:20.150 --> 00:12:23.320
Which means if you have a foot
in two different environments,

00:12:23.320 --> 00:12:26.260
how will they work together
when it comes to calendaring?

00:12:26.260 --> 00:12:28.650
We have tools to
bridge that gap,

00:12:28.650 --> 00:12:32.110
but we don't want that to
be a long-term solution.

00:12:32.110 --> 00:12:35.350
So we try to bring people
in as quickly as possible.

00:12:35.350 --> 00:12:38.740
We've had go lives as upwards
of 90,000 users coming

00:12:38.740 --> 00:12:40.310
into our environment
on the same day.

00:12:43.680 --> 00:12:45.960
So let's talk about the
people component of this.

00:12:45.960 --> 00:12:48.742
We talked through the technical
components of that switch,

00:12:48.742 --> 00:12:50.700
but what does that change
management look like?

00:12:50.700 --> 00:12:53.520
And I always say that a move
to G Suite, two-thirds of it

00:12:53.520 --> 00:12:54.910
is the people component.

00:12:54.910 --> 00:12:58.350
It's the change
management side of things.

00:12:58.350 --> 00:13:00.870
So this is our approach for how
we handle change management.

00:13:00.870 --> 00:13:02.550
I'm going to work on
the right side first,

00:13:02.550 --> 00:13:04.216
which is the
communications in training,

00:13:04.216 --> 00:13:07.290
and if there's nothing
else that you remember,

00:13:07.290 --> 00:13:09.600
it;s the word diversify.

00:13:09.600 --> 00:13:11.550
When it comes to creating
a change management

00:13:11.550 --> 00:13:15.000
plan for your staff at the
scale that we have been talking

00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:17.910
about, diversifying
your communications plan

00:13:17.910 --> 00:13:20.850
and diversifying your training
plan is super important.

00:13:20.850 --> 00:13:24.150
People hear and see
things in different ways

00:13:24.150 --> 00:13:25.650
that resonate with them.

00:13:25.650 --> 00:13:28.470
People will consume
training in different ways

00:13:28.470 --> 00:13:29.760
that resonate with them.

00:13:29.760 --> 00:13:31.830
Some folks will like
a setting like this

00:13:31.830 --> 00:13:33.750
and want to be
trained in person,

00:13:33.750 --> 00:13:35.460
and others just want
to sit at their desk

00:13:35.460 --> 00:13:37.920
and do it in a self-paced mode.

00:13:37.920 --> 00:13:41.460
So we want to make sure
that your plans for a move

00:13:41.460 --> 00:13:44.680
incorporate that diversity.

00:13:44.680 --> 00:13:47.680
We also want to do a bit
of organizational analysis

00:13:47.680 --> 00:13:50.140
to understand who are the
users in your organization

00:13:50.140 --> 00:13:53.320
that will be most impacted
by switch to G Suite.

00:13:53.320 --> 00:13:57.520
We pay special attention, for
example, to admin assistance. .

00:13:57.520 --> 00:14:00.820
They use the tools, especially
the messaging collaboration

00:14:00.820 --> 00:14:06.201
tools, very differently
than typical staff may.

00:14:06.201 --> 00:14:08.200
And we've paid special
attention on the training

00:14:08.200 --> 00:14:10.420
and the communication
with them, even

00:14:10.420 --> 00:14:13.990
being shoulder to shoulder
with them on important days

00:14:13.990 --> 00:14:16.440
when they're switching
to our environment.

00:14:16.440 --> 00:14:18.150
And also sponsorship
is super important.

00:14:18.150 --> 00:14:21.210
When we're talking about a
large organization like you

00:14:21.210 --> 00:14:25.620
saw today, at Verizon
or Colgate-Palmolive.

00:14:25.620 --> 00:14:27.540
Having that top down
sponsorship to say,

00:14:27.540 --> 00:14:30.730
this is why we are moving to
an environment like Google,

00:14:30.730 --> 00:14:33.030
this is what it means to
us as an organization,

00:14:33.030 --> 00:14:34.720
is super important.

00:14:34.720 --> 00:14:35.930
But there's a small nuance.

00:14:35.930 --> 00:14:38.860
Sponsorship also
includes the bottom up.

00:14:38.860 --> 00:14:41.100
Getting your staff
involved in the process.

00:14:41.100 --> 00:14:44.430
You don't want to dictate to
them that, hey, you're moving

00:14:44.430 --> 00:14:47.340
to a new environment, tough.

00:14:47.340 --> 00:14:49.136
You want them involved
in that process

00:14:49.136 --> 00:14:50.760
and I'll talk to you
in this next slide

00:14:50.760 --> 00:14:52.110
about what that means.

00:14:52.110 --> 00:14:54.120
We have a program called
Google Guides and what

00:14:54.120 --> 00:14:57.600
it is it's an old change
management best practice

00:14:57.600 --> 00:15:00.900
around creating change agents,
and we call them Google Guides.

00:15:00.900 --> 00:15:02.670
It's a fun thing to do.

00:15:02.670 --> 00:15:06.030
And in that early adopter
phase I talked about earlier,

00:15:06.030 --> 00:15:09.060
we typically recruit a
set of Google Guides.

00:15:09.060 --> 00:15:13.140
And these Google Guides are
growing into the environment,

00:15:13.140 --> 00:15:16.290
a month or more earlier
than the rest of the staff,

00:15:16.290 --> 00:15:20.970
and they're given
the opportunity

00:15:20.970 --> 00:15:25.140
to train on the toolset,
and start using it

00:15:25.140 --> 00:15:27.960
in their business workflows, and
advocate for the change that's

00:15:27.960 --> 00:15:28.460
happening.

00:15:28.460 --> 00:15:29.610
These are typically
folks who are

00:15:29.610 --> 00:15:31.320
using Google in
their personal lives

00:15:31.320 --> 00:15:33.420
or they've used it
at a previous job.

00:15:33.420 --> 00:15:36.570
They're very excited about
the move that's to take place

00:15:36.570 --> 00:15:39.180
and they want to help
their own team members make

00:15:39.180 --> 00:15:41.010
this transition successfully.

00:15:41.010 --> 00:15:43.350
So we pay special attention
to these Google Guides

00:15:43.350 --> 00:15:46.080
and make sure that they're
deployed out to key offices

00:15:46.080 --> 00:15:48.000
and environments
so that they can

00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:50.882
help us scale our transition.

00:15:50.882 --> 00:15:52.590
Of course it's, super
important that when

00:15:52.590 --> 00:15:54.780
you move to Google
that you make it fun.

00:15:54.780 --> 00:15:57.570
This isn't a typical
upgrade of your operating

00:15:57.570 --> 00:16:02.070
system or your usual upgrade
of your last environment.

00:16:02.070 --> 00:16:03.150
This move is exciting.

00:16:03.150 --> 00:16:06.210
I have never had so much fun
in this sort of an IT project

00:16:06.210 --> 00:16:08.520
as I have in deploying G Suite.

00:16:08.520 --> 00:16:11.300
Go lives have
cupcakes and parties.

00:16:11.300 --> 00:16:13.240
I remember a DJ booth once.

00:16:13.240 --> 00:16:17.160
You can make this a lot
of fun and fit the culture

00:16:17.160 --> 00:16:22.267
that you want to move
into as an organization.

00:16:22.267 --> 00:16:24.100
So I wanted to make
sure that you understood

00:16:24.100 --> 00:16:27.190
that we have tons of tools
behind this methodology to help

00:16:27.190 --> 00:16:28.810
you make this transition.

00:16:28.810 --> 00:16:30.640
Not only Google, but
our partners as well.

00:16:30.640 --> 00:16:33.730
They've built on top of
the base that we've created

00:16:33.730 --> 00:16:36.200
and we make it super easy for
you to make that transition.

00:16:36.200 --> 00:16:40.210
So for example, we have kickoff
templates and presentations

00:16:40.210 --> 00:16:45.040
that you can use to actually
get the change rolling.

00:16:45.040 --> 00:16:46.750
Even for your
communications planning we

00:16:46.750 --> 00:16:49.960
have posters and
template emails that you

00:16:49.960 --> 00:16:54.460
can use during your
communications outreach.

00:16:54.460 --> 00:17:00.320
In fact, last year we bought
a company called Synergize

00:17:00.320 --> 00:17:02.980
so that we could make self-paced
training available to you

00:17:02.980 --> 00:17:05.050
for free within the tool set.

00:17:05.050 --> 00:17:06.880
So when you have
thousands of users

00:17:06.880 --> 00:17:09.730
or even hundreds of users, you
don't have to bring all of them

00:17:09.730 --> 00:17:11.859
into a room to do
direct training.

00:17:11.859 --> 00:17:14.839
You can give them
self-paced tools as well.

00:17:14.839 --> 00:17:17.740
We even have a cool little
program for our top customers

00:17:17.740 --> 00:17:18.790
called Admin Gurus.

00:17:18.790 --> 00:17:22.210
Because I mentioned that we
pay special attention to admin

00:17:22.210 --> 00:17:24.220
assistants, we bring
admin assistants

00:17:24.220 --> 00:17:26.710
from Google who know how
to work in our environment

00:17:26.710 --> 00:17:29.020
and bring them to go
lives so that they

00:17:29.020 --> 00:17:31.650
can work with admin assistants
at other organizations.

00:17:31.650 --> 00:17:34.750
So you can expect for example,
Verizon and Colgate-Palmolive

00:17:34.750 --> 00:17:37.542
will have access to
those folks as well.

00:17:37.542 --> 00:17:39.250
We also have a very
deep learning center.

00:17:39.250 --> 00:17:41.680
So if you wanted to learn
specifically about the features

00:17:41.680 --> 00:17:44.140
and functionality of
our tools, individuals

00:17:44.140 --> 00:17:46.476
can go there and
learn more about that.

00:17:46.476 --> 00:17:48.100
So all these tools
are available to you

00:17:48.100 --> 00:17:49.690
and our partner
ecosystem has built

00:17:49.690 --> 00:17:52.390
on top of this with
their own added flavor

00:17:52.390 --> 00:17:56.282
to help you with
this transition.

00:17:56.282 --> 00:17:57.740
Let's talk about
the process piece.

00:17:57.740 --> 00:18:00.380
I mentioned that this is
a new concept that we've

00:18:00.380 --> 00:18:03.500
been working on for a few years
and it's deeply important to us

00:18:03.500 --> 00:18:07.700
that we don't just give
you a new set of tools

00:18:07.700 --> 00:18:12.320
where you continue to do the
old set of business processes.

00:18:12.320 --> 00:18:15.380
You may not do the same
workflow the same way when

00:18:15.380 --> 00:18:18.080
you have a new set of tools,
and we recognize that,

00:18:18.080 --> 00:18:19.960
and we want to help you
with that transition.

00:18:19.960 --> 00:18:20.810
And that's really
what we're trying

00:18:20.810 --> 00:18:23.390
to do when we address the
business process side.

00:18:23.390 --> 00:18:24.860
We recognize that
an organization

00:18:24.860 --> 00:18:28.970
that's moving to G Suite
has deep strategic goals

00:18:28.970 --> 00:18:29.580
in that move.

00:18:29.580 --> 00:18:33.560
It's no longer about
just saving money.

00:18:33.560 --> 00:18:35.940
They want to speed
their operations,

00:18:35.940 --> 00:18:37.760
or they want their
staff to be more

00:18:37.760 --> 00:18:40.220
innovative, they want to
increase communication,

00:18:40.220 --> 00:18:41.264
breakdown silos.

00:18:41.264 --> 00:18:42.680
But you can't just
tell your staff

00:18:42.680 --> 00:18:44.090
to go forth and be innovative.

00:18:44.090 --> 00:18:46.020
It just doesn't work that way.

00:18:46.020 --> 00:18:47.290
So what do you need to do?

00:18:47.290 --> 00:18:50.420
You need to focus and target
on the core behavioral changes

00:18:50.420 --> 00:18:53.530
that you're looking
for at scale.

00:18:53.530 --> 00:18:57.140
I put three examples up here.

00:18:57.140 --> 00:18:59.090
When I was working
with PWC, when

00:18:59.090 --> 00:19:01.040
they were moving, one
of the conversations

00:19:01.040 --> 00:19:02.690
that we'd been
having was how do we

00:19:02.690 --> 00:19:06.320
move from a conference
bridge and meeting ID culture

00:19:06.320 --> 00:19:07.850
to a video culture?

00:19:07.850 --> 00:19:11.810
Right A lot of folks would walk
around with laptops with tape

00:19:11.810 --> 00:19:13.280
on the cameras.

00:19:13.280 --> 00:19:15.435
I don't know if any
of you do that here.

00:19:15.435 --> 00:19:16.560
But how do we move to that?

00:19:16.560 --> 00:19:21.140
At Google, we actually do 60,000
video conferences every day,

00:19:21.140 --> 00:19:23.990
and we're not our
biggest customer.

00:19:23.990 --> 00:19:26.960
So we have customers who do more
than that even on our platform.

00:19:26.960 --> 00:19:28.460
But how do you get
into that zone?

00:19:28.460 --> 00:19:29.990
And that's a key
behavioral change

00:19:29.990 --> 00:19:33.240
that we have to influence.

00:19:33.240 --> 00:19:36.890
The point about
using the real time

00:19:36.890 --> 00:19:40.219
documents in Google Drive
and the editors that we have,

00:19:40.219 --> 00:19:42.260
that really allows you to
get to insights faster.

00:19:42.260 --> 00:19:44.010
You may actually have
a workflow right now

00:19:44.010 --> 00:19:47.690
that requires an analyst
creates a document,

00:19:47.690 --> 00:19:51.740
they check in that document
to a central environment

00:19:51.740 --> 00:19:54.800
or a repository, someone else
checks it out, they make edits,

00:19:54.800 --> 00:19:56.100
they check it back in.

00:19:56.100 --> 00:19:59.360
Well, can we help you
there change that workflow

00:19:59.360 --> 00:20:00.740
to be more real time?

00:20:00.740 --> 00:20:02.840
And that's something that
our tools can help with.

00:20:02.840 --> 00:20:04.910
The point about mobile
is interesting too.

00:20:04.910 --> 00:20:07.757
We're of course, all in mobile.

00:20:07.757 --> 00:20:09.590
We're all in a mobile
environment right now,

00:20:09.590 --> 00:20:12.470
but if all you are doing is
checking your mail and calendar

00:20:12.470 --> 00:20:15.410
or mobile, you're not getting
the most benefit out of it.

00:20:15.410 --> 00:20:17.630
What are the possibilities
of the business processes

00:20:17.630 --> 00:20:19.838
and workflows you can
influence if you could actually

00:20:19.838 --> 00:20:22.700
contribute content
through mobile,

00:20:22.700 --> 00:20:25.890
or take surveys through
your mobile environment,

00:20:25.890 --> 00:20:28.420
or add to presentations
with mobile,

00:20:28.420 --> 00:20:30.170
or even give the
presentation with mobile?

00:20:30.170 --> 00:20:32.270
I should have done that.

00:20:32.270 --> 00:20:35.210
So these are options that
you might think about

00:20:35.210 --> 00:20:37.856
and how they might transform
your business if you think

00:20:37.856 --> 00:20:39.980
about mobile a little bit
differently when you have

00:20:39.980 --> 00:20:42.980
access to tools like G Suite.

00:20:42.980 --> 00:20:45.110
One of the ways that
we try and influence

00:20:45.110 --> 00:20:47.300
this behavioral change
is a tool we have

00:20:47.300 --> 00:20:51.140
called the Transformation Lab.

00:20:51.140 --> 00:20:52.160
It's a workshop.

00:20:52.160 --> 00:20:54.200
It's a working
session where I like

00:20:54.200 --> 00:20:57.530
to say we spend half
the time listening

00:20:57.530 --> 00:20:59.840
and then the other
half of the time

00:20:59.840 --> 00:21:04.700
working shoulder to shoulder
with an organization staff

00:21:04.700 --> 00:21:07.670
to envision how you
would apply Google

00:21:07.670 --> 00:21:10.200
tools to their existing
business process.

00:21:10.200 --> 00:21:16.610
So for example, we work
a lot with organizations

00:21:16.610 --> 00:21:19.035
that you may have
a HR department

00:21:19.035 --> 00:21:19.910
in your organization.

00:21:19.910 --> 00:21:22.550
If we were to do a
Transformation Lab with the HR

00:21:22.550 --> 00:21:25.160
department, think about
all the business processes

00:21:25.160 --> 00:21:27.740
that they currently engage
in that could benefit

00:21:27.740 --> 00:21:29.320
from a tool like G Suite.

00:21:29.320 --> 00:21:34.670
I remember we did one with
a large group of HR folks

00:21:34.670 --> 00:21:37.220
and they came up with the
terrific idea of doing

00:21:37.220 --> 00:21:40.160
interviews using Hangouts,
and that's something

00:21:40.160 --> 00:21:41.330
that we took for granted.

00:21:41.330 --> 00:21:43.850
I never even thought
to just bring it up

00:21:43.850 --> 00:21:45.770
because we do interviews
using Hangouts

00:21:45.770 --> 00:21:48.230
all the time at Google, but
this particular organization

00:21:48.230 --> 00:21:49.100
did not.

00:21:49.100 --> 00:21:52.190
And so they decided to switch
their second round interviews

00:21:52.190 --> 00:21:52.944
to use Hangouts.

00:21:52.944 --> 00:21:54.860
And what that allowed
them to do is save money

00:21:54.860 --> 00:21:57.620
on scheduling the
right interviewee

00:21:57.620 --> 00:22:00.754
with the right interviewer,
bringing multiple panels

00:22:00.754 --> 00:22:03.170
together so they could now
expand how they did interviews,

00:22:03.170 --> 00:22:05.419
not just one on ones, but
they could create panels now

00:22:05.419 --> 00:22:06.442
as well.

00:22:06.442 --> 00:22:08.900
So it just changed the approach
to that particular business

00:22:08.900 --> 00:22:09.400
process.

00:22:12.397 --> 00:22:13.980
So with the
Transformation Lab, I I'll

00:22:13.980 --> 00:22:15.420
give you another example here.

00:22:15.420 --> 00:22:18.360
We worked with
Brigs and Stratton.

00:22:18.360 --> 00:22:21.390
We brought in a few folks
from the manufacturing team

00:22:21.390 --> 00:22:25.050
who are responsible
for their processes

00:22:25.050 --> 00:22:26.820
out on the shop floor.

00:22:26.820 --> 00:22:30.032
And they had an
example where what

00:22:30.032 --> 00:22:31.740
would happen is when
there was a question

00:22:31.740 --> 00:22:35.610
about a particular
part or an assembly,

00:22:35.610 --> 00:22:37.530
they would have
to stop the belt,

00:22:37.530 --> 00:22:40.560
go over and research it
in a bunch of binders,

00:22:40.560 --> 00:22:43.470
and then ask somebody or call
somebody if they needed help,

00:22:43.470 --> 00:22:46.120
and then come back and
then restart that process.

00:22:46.120 --> 00:22:49.710
And that was a very
expensive, error prone,

00:22:49.710 --> 00:22:52.770
safety hazard type
of business process.

00:22:52.770 --> 00:22:56.917
So what we helped them with is
someone in the Transformation

00:22:56.917 --> 00:22:59.250
Lab came up with the idea of
let's create a Google site.

00:22:59.250 --> 00:23:02.150
Now, creating a Google site
in G Suite is super simple.

00:23:02.150 --> 00:23:05.550
It does not require IT to go
in and set up servers for you

00:23:05.550 --> 00:23:08.010
or set up environments, you
just go ahead and create it.

00:23:08.010 --> 00:23:09.960
So that the person
with the best knowledge

00:23:09.960 --> 00:23:11.712
can create a site very easily.

00:23:11.712 --> 00:23:13.170
So he created a
Google site, and he

00:23:13.170 --> 00:23:15.120
started scanning
some of the manuals

00:23:15.120 --> 00:23:18.030
that they had, and put
that on Google Drive

00:23:18.030 --> 00:23:19.890
and linked it into the site.

00:23:19.890 --> 00:23:22.410
So then he went
a little further.

00:23:22.410 --> 00:23:24.900
He created access
to that Google site

00:23:24.900 --> 00:23:27.870
on the shop floor itself
using a Chromebook.

00:23:27.870 --> 00:23:31.410
Now he didn't have to go to the
back office to look content up,

00:23:31.410 --> 00:23:34.290
you could actually just
go to the Chromebook,

00:23:34.290 --> 00:23:37.320
do a search on Drive for
particular part numbers,

00:23:37.320 --> 00:23:40.950
because we OCR in Drive, and
look up those instructions

00:23:40.950 --> 00:23:42.680
very quickly.

00:23:42.680 --> 00:23:45.020
I actually had one customer
in a Transformation Lab

00:23:45.020 --> 00:23:46.860
who used this exact
same scenario,

00:23:46.860 --> 00:23:48.900
but they went a
little bit further.

00:23:48.900 --> 00:23:52.860
What they did was they got
the manufacturing teams

00:23:52.860 --> 00:23:56.230
to put a QR code
stickers on their parts.

00:23:56.230 --> 00:23:58.230
And the reason for that
is everything in G Suite

00:23:58.230 --> 00:23:58.860
is a link.

00:23:58.860 --> 00:24:01.590
It's an awesome feature.

00:24:01.590 --> 00:24:05.280
So a Google Doc is a
link, a Hangout is a link,

00:24:05.280 --> 00:24:08.280
an individual sheet in
a spreadsheet is a link,

00:24:08.280 --> 00:24:10.300
and you can link to
these things directly.

00:24:10.300 --> 00:24:12.690
So when this individual
started putting stickers

00:24:12.690 --> 00:24:15.540
on their parts, when they
sent maintenance staff over,

00:24:15.540 --> 00:24:17.130
they could scan
the QR code, which

00:24:17.130 --> 00:24:19.860
in fact, was just a link
to the specific instruction

00:24:19.860 --> 00:24:21.690
manual for that part.

00:24:21.690 --> 00:24:25.320
And that saved folks from having
to lug binders and suitcases

00:24:25.320 --> 00:24:29.160
over just to figure out
exactly what might be wrong.

00:24:29.160 --> 00:24:30.900
So think about those
business processes

00:24:30.900 --> 00:24:31.830
that could be innovated on.

00:24:31.830 --> 00:24:33.720
You might just not do
them the exact same way

00:24:33.720 --> 00:24:37.860
if you had a new set of
tools helping your business.

00:24:37.860 --> 00:24:39.780
The other thing is, we
can't do Transformation

00:24:39.780 --> 00:24:43.740
Labs with all 10,000
people at an organization.

00:24:43.740 --> 00:24:44.754
That can take some time.

00:24:44.754 --> 00:24:46.170
We'd certainly
hope you'd do that,

00:24:46.170 --> 00:24:48.510
but we want to help
folks at scale too,

00:24:48.510 --> 00:24:51.000
and there are things that
we call scale solutions that

00:24:51.000 --> 00:24:52.360
can be deployed.

00:24:52.360 --> 00:24:54.630
So I'll give you some examples.

00:24:54.630 --> 00:24:57.180
Organizations do a lot of
project management, especially

00:24:57.180 --> 00:24:58.590
knowledge workers.

00:24:58.590 --> 00:25:01.014
So it's very easy for you to
create a project management

00:25:01.014 --> 00:25:02.430
template in Google
Sheets and make

00:25:02.430 --> 00:25:04.410
that available to
your whole domain

00:25:04.410 --> 00:25:06.567
so that anyone can
look up in Drive,

00:25:06.567 --> 00:25:09.150
project management, and come up
with lots of different project

00:25:09.150 --> 00:25:11.370
management templates,
or any template

00:25:11.370 --> 00:25:13.170
that you can set up for them.

00:25:13.170 --> 00:25:15.640
Another template would be
your corporate template

00:25:15.640 --> 00:25:16.450
on Google Slides.

00:25:16.450 --> 00:25:18.000
You now have the
ability to create

00:25:18.000 --> 00:25:20.550
a corporate branded
template so that folks

00:25:20.550 --> 00:25:22.110
aren't starting
from scratch when

00:25:22.110 --> 00:25:24.270
they're firing up a new tool.

00:25:24.270 --> 00:25:27.690
You may have templates
for proposals or templates

00:25:27.690 --> 00:25:29.370
for working with vendors.

00:25:29.370 --> 00:25:31.285
These all can be
converted and brought

00:25:31.285 --> 00:25:33.660
into the environment to make
it super easy for your users

00:25:33.660 --> 00:25:35.830
to adopt.

00:25:35.830 --> 00:25:36.830
Google Sites as well.

00:25:36.830 --> 00:25:40.060
You can create templates
with your corporate branding

00:25:40.060 --> 00:25:43.710
on how to create a site that
has your corporate branding

00:25:43.710 --> 00:25:46.270
and then inspires users
on how to use it properly.

00:25:46.270 --> 00:25:48.080
I work with the
state of Colorado

00:25:48.080 --> 00:25:50.650
and they use this
to great effect

00:25:50.650 --> 00:25:53.890
in saving money on
publishing sites, both

00:25:53.890 --> 00:25:59.330
internally and externally
using Google Sites.

00:25:59.330 --> 00:26:02.330
If you're struggling to think
about what business processes

00:26:02.330 --> 00:26:05.630
you can impact using
G Suite, we tried

00:26:05.630 --> 00:26:07.430
to make that a little
easier for you.

00:26:07.430 --> 00:26:09.620
So I mentioned the HR
use case, but there's

00:26:09.620 --> 00:26:12.530
lots of areas within your
business that you can impact.

00:26:12.530 --> 00:26:15.380
So we've created a
website, a public website,

00:26:15.380 --> 00:26:17.820
that you can go to, even
with your mobile phones now.

00:26:17.820 --> 00:26:21.512
G.co/transformationgallery,
and you can look up all

00:26:21.512 --> 00:26:23.720
the different business
processes that you might think

00:26:23.720 --> 00:26:26.984
differently about if you
were to use a G Suite tool.

00:26:26.984 --> 00:26:28.400
These are all
learnings that we've

00:26:28.400 --> 00:26:31.094
gathered from doing hundreds
of Transformation Labs

00:26:31.094 --> 00:26:32.510
and we decided to
publish them out

00:26:32.510 --> 00:26:35.600
so that we can inspire other
teams that might be interested.

00:26:35.600 --> 00:26:38.820
So definitely check that out.

00:26:38.820 --> 00:26:40.640
So I want to hand
off to Heather here

00:26:40.640 --> 00:26:43.970
to talk to you about
the long term process

00:26:43.970 --> 00:26:47.480
and how we like to stay engaged
once you're on the platform.

00:26:47.480 --> 00:26:47.980
Heather.

00:26:47.980 --> 00:26:49.614
HEATHER TRAHER: Hi, everyone.

00:26:49.614 --> 00:26:51.530
So you've seen a little
bit about the planning

00:26:51.530 --> 00:26:54.050
and the change management that
goes into moving to G Suite,

00:26:54.050 --> 00:26:55.550
and so now I'd like
to just walk you

00:26:55.550 --> 00:26:57.260
through what happens
after you deploy.

00:26:57.260 --> 00:26:59.840
Like Amar was saying, this is
not a cut and run relationship.

00:26:59.840 --> 00:27:01.506
We really want to
stay with you and work

00:27:01.506 --> 00:27:03.650
with you for the long haul.

00:27:03.650 --> 00:27:06.500
And in addition to unlocking
the benefits of collaboration

00:27:06.500 --> 00:27:08.780
and other features for
your end users in G Suite,

00:27:08.780 --> 00:27:10.550
we also want to
unlock ways to help

00:27:10.550 --> 00:27:13.100
you show that the choice you
made to transition to Google

00:27:13.100 --> 00:27:13.724
is paying off.

00:27:13.724 --> 00:27:15.140
So we want to make
sure you're are

00:27:15.140 --> 00:27:17.960
able to see when and how
people are working better

00:27:17.960 --> 00:27:19.910
together in your
organization and really show

00:27:19.910 --> 00:27:21.394
the impact of these new tools.

00:27:21.394 --> 00:27:23.060
And if you see there's
some things you'd

00:27:23.060 --> 00:27:25.640
like to improve as you're making
the transition to G Suite,

00:27:25.640 --> 00:27:27.260
or even just about the
products themselves,

00:27:27.260 --> 00:27:29.635
we really want to make sure
that you have the opportunity

00:27:29.635 --> 00:27:34.260
to give us that feedback so that
we can make things go better.

00:27:34.260 --> 00:27:36.060
Now as your users
are adopting G Suite,

00:27:36.060 --> 00:27:38.880
it's critical to measure
the impact on both the users

00:27:38.880 --> 00:27:40.047
and on your business.

00:27:40.047 --> 00:27:42.630
You had a goal that you set when
you decide to go with Google,

00:27:42.630 --> 00:27:45.300
and we want to make sure you
can measure your effectiveness,

00:27:45.300 --> 00:27:47.674
and we think about measuring
this in terms of these three

00:27:47.674 --> 00:27:50.742
buckets-- adoption,
collaboration, and impact.

00:27:50.742 --> 00:27:52.200
Adoption is pretty
straightforward.

00:27:52.200 --> 00:27:55.260
Simply, how many of your users
are utilizing the applications

00:27:55.260 --> 00:27:57.300
within G Suite,
and are those uses

00:27:57.300 --> 00:27:59.220
numbers for non-Google
tools going down?

00:27:59.220 --> 00:28:01.710
So for example, you could
measure Hangouts adoption as

00:28:01.710 --> 00:28:05.280
compared to web sharing
or a dial-in bridge tool.

00:28:05.280 --> 00:28:07.270
For collaboration,
how are your users

00:28:07.270 --> 00:28:09.900
engaging with each other
and the applications?

00:28:09.900 --> 00:28:12.500
Are they creating content
natively in G Suite?

00:28:12.500 --> 00:28:15.420
Or are they're still using
it more as a storage drive?

00:28:15.420 --> 00:28:17.550
Are people sharing
content and collaborating

00:28:17.550 --> 00:28:20.070
with each other, and really,
which features are taking off

00:28:20.070 --> 00:28:22.380
and having an impact
in your business?

00:28:22.380 --> 00:28:25.200
Which brings me to impact, which
is once you know your users are

00:28:25.200 --> 00:28:27.630
in the products and
engaging, how is this really

00:28:27.630 --> 00:28:29.720
impacting your bottom
line and the users day

00:28:29.720 --> 00:28:30.781
to day experience?

00:28:30.781 --> 00:28:32.280
So here you're going
to want to look

00:28:32.280 --> 00:28:34.860
at the productivity,
collaboration,

00:28:34.860 --> 00:28:37.470
flexibility, and where people
are getting their work done,

00:28:37.470 --> 00:28:40.330
and the device are using
to get that work done,

00:28:40.330 --> 00:28:43.320
and even innovations in how they
feel they are doing their jobs.

00:28:43.320 --> 00:28:46.150
So you're going to find a lot
of that data in the C panel.

00:28:46.150 --> 00:28:48.090
You can also leverage
the reporting API,

00:28:48.090 --> 00:28:51.120
and analyze data in BigQuery,
and create visualizations

00:28:51.120 --> 00:28:52.710
in Data Studio.

00:28:52.710 --> 00:28:54.960
For that rich use case
and satisfaction data

00:28:54.960 --> 00:28:57.440
I was mentioning, you
could turn to Google Forms

00:28:57.440 --> 00:28:59.220
and create a survey
to really understand

00:28:59.220 --> 00:29:01.500
how your end users are
feeling about the tools.

00:29:01.500 --> 00:29:03.670
Are they feeling as though
they're working faster?

00:29:03.670 --> 00:29:06.000
Are they proud of the workflows
that they've developed?

00:29:06.000 --> 00:29:07.500
We've observed in
a lot of customers

00:29:07.500 --> 00:29:09.583
that we've worked with,
that on average, employees

00:29:09.583 --> 00:29:11.730
have felt they've saved
upwards of two hours

00:29:11.730 --> 00:29:14.700
a week once they switch to these
tools, which in aggregate is

00:29:14.700 --> 00:29:16.866
actually quite a big deal
and it can be a great data

00:29:16.866 --> 00:29:18.720
point for you proving
the case of going

00:29:18.720 --> 00:29:21.520
Google being a success.

00:29:21.520 --> 00:29:23.320
Amar said it, I'm
going to say it again.

00:29:23.320 --> 00:29:25.930
We're here as your
long-term partner.

00:29:25.930 --> 00:29:27.630
So when you go
live with G Suite,

00:29:27.630 --> 00:29:29.500
you're added automatically
to a community

00:29:29.500 --> 00:29:31.364
of your peers, the
Cloud Connect Platform,

00:29:31.364 --> 00:29:33.280
where you're going to
meet other customers who

00:29:33.280 --> 00:29:34.937
are trying to solve
similar problems,

00:29:34.937 --> 00:29:36.520
as well as meet
Googlers who are going

00:29:36.520 --> 00:29:39.340
to interact with you
there on a regular basis.

00:29:39.340 --> 00:29:41.650
Our teams also provide
opportunities for networking

00:29:41.650 --> 00:29:44.000
and problem solving
through in-person meet-ups,

00:29:44.000 --> 00:29:45.940
through advisory boards,
and opportunities

00:29:45.940 --> 00:29:49.420
to try out new features
and provide early feedback.

00:29:49.420 --> 00:29:52.060
We have user research programs,
early access, trusted tester

00:29:52.060 --> 00:29:52.942
programs.

00:29:52.942 --> 00:29:55.150
And we want to come to you
to learn as much as we can

00:29:55.150 --> 00:29:57.430
about your use cases and
have your needs really

00:29:57.430 --> 00:30:00.679
influence our product roadmap.

00:30:00.679 --> 00:30:02.720
I want to take a minute
to talk a little bit more

00:30:02.720 --> 00:30:04.300
about our user experience team.

00:30:04.300 --> 00:30:07.550
That's the team where
I spend my days.

00:30:07.550 --> 00:30:09.500
So across G Suite
we have a large team

00:30:09.500 --> 00:30:12.020
of both qualitative and
quantitative researchers

00:30:12.020 --> 00:30:14.150
who are embedded on
the product team.

00:30:14.150 --> 00:30:16.430
They work day to day
with design, engineering,

00:30:16.430 --> 00:30:18.680
and product management
teams, and really the goal

00:30:18.680 --> 00:30:21.140
is to have them function
as the voice of our users

00:30:21.140 --> 00:30:23.330
or our customers as we're
designing and executing

00:30:23.330 --> 00:30:24.826
our new features.

00:30:24.826 --> 00:30:26.450
Our teams really
focus on understanding

00:30:26.450 --> 00:30:28.880
the needs of a range
of enterprise customers

00:30:28.880 --> 00:30:31.444
and feeding that information
back into the product roadmaps.

00:30:31.444 --> 00:30:33.110
So we really want to
spend time with you

00:30:33.110 --> 00:30:36.747
to hear about how we can best
help you with our products.

00:30:36.747 --> 00:30:39.080
I'll take him in and mention
some of the common research

00:30:39.080 --> 00:30:41.990
methods that we might use with
you if you were to participate

00:30:41.990 --> 00:30:43.140
in something like this.

00:30:43.140 --> 00:30:45.590
So the first one
is field research.

00:30:45.590 --> 00:30:48.540
You can imagine this as a sort
of day in a life exercise,

00:30:48.540 --> 00:30:50.210
or even a fly on a
wall type activity

00:30:50.210 --> 00:30:52.520
where Google researchers
and other Google staff

00:30:52.520 --> 00:30:54.980
will come on site,
spend time with you,

00:30:54.980 --> 00:30:57.332
observe people in their
natural work environment,

00:30:57.332 --> 00:30:58.790
whether that be a
knowledge worker,

00:30:58.790 --> 00:31:00.800
whether that be somebody
on the shop floor,

00:31:00.800 --> 00:31:03.080
and really just become
familiar with the key workflows

00:31:03.080 --> 00:31:04.820
and use cases for your business.

00:31:04.820 --> 00:31:07.100
And those might be use
cases where you're already

00:31:07.100 --> 00:31:09.746
using Google products, or those
might be holes in our offering

00:31:09.746 --> 00:31:11.120
right now, and we
can sort of get

00:31:11.120 --> 00:31:12.980
a sense for what's
not working for you

00:31:12.980 --> 00:31:16.590
and bring that back to
the product team as well.

00:31:16.590 --> 00:31:18.230
We also do a lot of
end user surveys.

00:31:18.230 --> 00:31:20.120
These are targeted,
simple surveys just

00:31:20.120 --> 00:31:23.120
to help learn more about the
scope and size of use cases,

00:31:23.120 --> 00:31:25.820
or interest in
potential features

00:31:25.820 --> 00:31:29.300
that are being considered
for our roadmap.

00:31:29.300 --> 00:31:31.610
Once we have an idea
that we think has legs,

00:31:31.610 --> 00:31:34.580
we want to make sure that we do
not go off and build something

00:31:34.580 --> 00:31:36.039
without checking
with our customers

00:31:36.039 --> 00:31:38.330
and making sure this is really
hitting the need that we

00:31:38.330 --> 00:31:40.080
had in mind when we
started developing it.

00:31:40.080 --> 00:31:42.290
So we're going to do
some concept testing.

00:31:42.290 --> 00:31:44.537
Sometimes this is as simple
as showing you sketches

00:31:44.537 --> 00:31:46.370
and just having you
talk it through with us.

00:31:46.370 --> 00:31:48.953
Sometimes we may have something
that's more of a high fidelity

00:31:48.953 --> 00:31:50.554
mock up and get your feedback.

00:31:50.554 --> 00:31:52.970
And then once something is
committed, it's on the roadmap,

00:31:52.970 --> 00:31:55.340
we know it's going to be
launching, it becomes really

00:31:55.340 --> 00:31:58.460
critical to do that one on
one, really hands on usability

00:31:58.460 --> 00:32:00.985
testing with you and make sure
that these things that we are

00:32:00.985 --> 00:32:02.360
used to looking
at, are really as

00:32:02.360 --> 00:32:04.401
easy to use as they should
be before they get out

00:32:04.401 --> 00:32:06.699
in the market.

00:32:06.699 --> 00:32:08.240
So a two-way
relationship with Google

00:32:08.240 --> 00:32:11.397
is going to lead to less time
on support issues for you

00:32:11.397 --> 00:32:13.730
and it's going to lead to
better solutions for everybody

00:32:13.730 --> 00:32:15.860
in our enterprise community.

00:32:15.860 --> 00:32:18.320
So there's lots of different
channels for your feedback.

00:32:18.320 --> 00:32:21.274
We've spoken about the connect
community and user research.

00:32:21.274 --> 00:32:22.940
There's also the
Trusted Tester Program,

00:32:22.940 --> 00:32:25.369
which is going to allow
you to look at products

00:32:25.369 --> 00:32:27.410
and get sort of a sneak
peek and live with things

00:32:27.410 --> 00:32:29.659
over a period of time before
they launch to the larger

00:32:29.659 --> 00:32:31.857
population.

00:32:31.857 --> 00:32:33.690
Something new that we've
recently kicked off

00:32:33.690 --> 00:32:36.004
is our Enterprise
Customer Research Panel.

00:32:36.004 --> 00:32:37.920
So this is something
that's going to allow you

00:32:37.920 --> 00:32:39.330
and the end users
at your company

00:32:39.330 --> 00:32:41.700
to have opportunities to
give really regular feedback

00:32:41.700 --> 00:32:43.616
to Google on how
you work, what you

00:32:43.616 --> 00:32:45.240
like to see in our
products, and again,

00:32:45.240 --> 00:32:46.700
early feedback on product ideas.

00:32:46.700 --> 00:32:48.450
This is something
that's a little bit more

00:32:48.450 --> 00:32:50.033
formalized than some
of the activities

00:32:50.033 --> 00:32:51.220
that I mentioned earlier.

00:32:51.220 --> 00:32:53.070
And so, we'd love
to have anyone who

00:32:53.070 --> 00:32:55.470
might be interested,
contact us, sign up

00:32:55.470 --> 00:32:58.230
to participate so you can
try out some of these things

00:32:58.230 --> 00:32:59.314
that we've gone over.

00:32:59.314 --> 00:33:01.230
We really do just want
to spend time with you,

00:33:01.230 --> 00:33:02.271
understanding your needs.

00:33:02.271 --> 00:33:03.870
Not everybody works
like Google and we

00:33:03.870 --> 00:33:06.420
want to be smarter about
what we can be building.

00:33:06.420 --> 00:33:08.790
This is a program we
started scaling in Q4

00:33:08.790 --> 00:33:13.402
and we're ramping up day by day.

00:33:13.402 --> 00:33:15.110
And so, with that, I
want to hand it over

00:33:15.110 --> 00:33:17.210
to Flint, who can give you
a bit of a first person

00:33:17.210 --> 00:33:19.592
perspective on his
experiences going Google

00:33:19.592 --> 00:33:20.675
with the State of Wyoming.

00:33:23.227 --> 00:33:24.060
FLINT WATERS: Great.

00:33:24.060 --> 00:33:26.920
Thank you.

00:33:26.920 --> 00:33:27.420
Go ahead.

00:33:27.420 --> 00:33:28.248
You have the next--

00:33:30.880 --> 00:33:32.320
oh, there we go.

00:33:32.320 --> 00:33:33.680
I'm Flint waters.

00:33:33.680 --> 00:33:35.980
I was the CIO for
the State of Wyoming

00:33:35.980 --> 00:33:39.340
for five and a half years, and
Wyoming was the first state

00:33:39.340 --> 00:33:41.150
to deploy Google.

00:33:41.150 --> 00:33:43.120
And I wanted to have
this image of cowboy

00:33:43.120 --> 00:33:46.990
because really nothing instills
a view of technical innovation

00:33:46.990 --> 00:33:49.230
more so than the cowboy.

00:33:49.230 --> 00:33:49.810
Right?

00:33:49.810 --> 00:33:51.040
Nothing?

00:33:51.040 --> 00:33:51.910
Tough room.

00:33:51.910 --> 00:33:52.410
All

00:33:52.410 --> 00:33:56.860
Right, one of the things that we
took on in the State of Wyoming

00:33:56.860 --> 00:34:01.970
was we have these vast
IT silos structure

00:34:01.970 --> 00:34:03.550
throughout all of the agencies.

00:34:03.550 --> 00:34:06.910
And in many cases, an
agency in state government

00:34:06.910 --> 00:34:07.870
is its own empire.

00:34:07.870 --> 00:34:10.440
So department of health,
and transportation,

00:34:10.440 --> 00:34:13.000
and corrections, and the
law enforcement groups,

00:34:13.000 --> 00:34:14.230
the attorneys.

00:34:14.230 --> 00:34:19.870
They all ran their own IT stacks
and in various stages of decay.

00:34:19.870 --> 00:34:21.861
Almost nothing was modern.

00:34:21.861 --> 00:34:23.110
Almost nothing was up to date.

00:34:23.110 --> 00:34:28.360
In fact, the day that I walked
in, brand new job of CIO.

00:34:28.360 --> 00:34:31.079
I had retired from the
state a few years earlier.

00:34:31.079 --> 00:34:32.620
I ran the Internet
Crimes Task Force.

00:34:32.620 --> 00:34:36.219
So I ran high tech crime
investigations rescuing kids.

00:34:36.219 --> 00:34:37.945
And when Governor
Mead got in, he

00:34:37.945 --> 00:34:39.900
asked me to come
back as the CIO.

00:34:39.900 --> 00:34:42.310
The day that I walked
in, they handed me

00:34:42.310 --> 00:34:44.559
a Windows laptop
and a BlackBerry,

00:34:44.559 --> 00:34:45.850
and they said this is the norm.

00:34:45.850 --> 00:34:47.500
This is how we do business.

00:34:47.500 --> 00:34:49.570
And I said well,
that's OK, but I'm

00:34:49.570 --> 00:34:51.550
far more effective on a Mac.

00:34:51.550 --> 00:34:53.799
And you could hear the hush
fall over the building.

00:34:53.799 --> 00:34:54.900
It was like [GASP]--

00:34:54.900 --> 00:34:57.110
this gasping across [INAUDIBLE].

00:34:57.110 --> 00:34:59.470
I said, so I'm going
to need an iPhone.

00:34:59.470 --> 00:35:02.800
And the prior CIO had already
contracted with Google

00:35:02.800 --> 00:35:03.650
to bring in G Suite.

00:35:06.170 --> 00:35:07.250
They were just deploying.

00:35:07.250 --> 00:35:10.580
In fact, the week I started
was their first rollout

00:35:10.580 --> 00:35:12.217
for doing adoption.

00:35:12.217 --> 00:35:14.800
And the governor tasked me with
going in, doing some research,

00:35:14.800 --> 00:35:16.110
figure out if this
was a smart move,

00:35:16.110 --> 00:35:18.030
that this was good for
the State of Wyoming,

00:35:18.030 --> 00:35:21.150
and how could we leverage this
to really make a difference?

00:35:21.150 --> 00:35:25.350
His critical focus was impacting
quality of life for citizens.

00:35:25.350 --> 00:35:29.062
And we had done a lot of work
in the past trying to achieve

00:35:29.062 --> 00:35:30.270
that goal through technology.

00:35:30.270 --> 00:35:31.992
So that was his directive to me.

00:35:31.992 --> 00:35:33.450
And we dove in and
started looking,

00:35:33.450 --> 00:35:37.290
and I made quite a few pretty
good sized mistakes early on.

00:35:37.290 --> 00:35:40.170
There were a lot of challenges
we faced, and none of them

00:35:40.170 --> 00:35:44.580
were technological, at least
none tech related to Google.

00:35:44.580 --> 00:35:48.960
The tech went really easy,
but we had not really

00:35:48.960 --> 00:35:52.830
expected what was going to
happen with regards to the user

00:35:52.830 --> 00:35:56.820
adoption, to the scope and
potential of what we could do

00:35:56.820 --> 00:35:59.280
with these tools, and
I had not properly

00:35:59.280 --> 00:36:02.040
engaged some of the most
important innovators

00:36:02.040 --> 00:36:03.900
within state government.

00:36:03.900 --> 00:36:07.920
Now, as we took off down this
road, before it was over,

00:36:07.920 --> 00:36:11.910
we consolidated all of IT,
we brought all those servers

00:36:11.910 --> 00:36:14.380
out of the hallways and out
of the individual stacks

00:36:14.380 --> 00:36:15.546
within agencies.

00:36:15.546 --> 00:36:17.670
We brought all that in and
created an enterprise IT

00:36:17.670 --> 00:36:18.840
agency.

00:36:18.840 --> 00:36:21.520
We did that in about 18 months.

00:36:21.520 --> 00:36:24.440
When it was done, we
closed our data centers.

00:36:24.440 --> 00:36:27.320
We actually shifted so that
folks could operate any time,

00:36:27.320 --> 00:36:30.040
anywhere, from any device.

00:36:30.040 --> 00:36:31.150
That was a big lift.

00:36:31.150 --> 00:36:33.670
It was an interesting
challenge to take on,

00:36:33.670 --> 00:36:37.430
but it also really helped
cement the relationship,

00:36:37.430 --> 00:36:39.660
the partnership that
we had with Google.

00:36:39.660 --> 00:36:43.260
And I'll just give
you a few examples.

00:36:43.260 --> 00:36:45.500
When we first announced
what we were doing,

00:36:45.500 --> 00:36:47.570
the training work that
we did on the front end,

00:36:47.570 --> 00:36:50.150
it was about email and calendar.

00:36:50.150 --> 00:36:53.330
And there were like 13 email
systems around the state.

00:36:53.330 --> 00:36:55.790
There was no central directory.

00:36:55.790 --> 00:36:58.580
There was no single sign.

00:36:58.580 --> 00:37:00.530
We started from way behind zero.

00:37:00.530 --> 00:37:03.140
So there were a lot
of pretty heavy lifts

00:37:03.140 --> 00:37:04.970
that we had to take
off from the beginning,

00:37:04.970 --> 00:37:07.760
and I didn't really
expect what was going

00:37:07.760 --> 00:37:12.464
to happen in terms of some of
the more innovative additions

00:37:12.464 --> 00:37:13.880
we had on our team
that they would

00:37:13.880 --> 00:37:15.800
start doing inside Google.

00:37:15.800 --> 00:37:18.590
So if you take on
this journey, I

00:37:18.590 --> 00:37:21.740
would definitely
look at that outline

00:37:21.740 --> 00:37:23.690
that they talked
about for adoption

00:37:23.690 --> 00:37:26.330
because it would have made
a lot of difference for us.

00:37:26.330 --> 00:37:29.000
One of the things that
I wish I had done,

00:37:29.000 --> 00:37:31.460
I wish I had gone through
and worked out my retention

00:37:31.460 --> 00:37:32.960
schedules in advance.

00:37:32.960 --> 00:37:36.380
I wish I had gotten rid of
a lot of the old information

00:37:36.380 --> 00:37:39.200
that was laying around, instead
of going in and going to all

00:37:39.200 --> 00:37:40.910
the work to load
it, get everything

00:37:40.910 --> 00:37:43.370
built in the enterprise, and
then have folks say OK, great.

00:37:43.370 --> 00:37:44.828
Now can you have
it delete anything

00:37:44.828 --> 00:37:46.590
that was two years old?

00:37:46.590 --> 00:37:47.450
OK.

00:37:47.450 --> 00:37:49.290
I spent a lot of
time doing that.

00:37:49.290 --> 00:37:51.200
I should have prepared
ahead of time.

00:37:51.200 --> 00:37:53.700
I really should have engaged--

00:37:53.700 --> 00:37:56.120
let's see, how do I
say this correctly?

00:37:56.120 --> 00:38:00.380
Within every IT organization,
there were experts.

00:38:00.380 --> 00:38:02.450
They weren't
necessarily IT experts,

00:38:02.450 --> 00:38:05.600
but they were folks that
had invested in themselves

00:38:05.600 --> 00:38:07.370
and were the best
in their agency

00:38:07.370 --> 00:38:10.330
at leveraging the
tools they had.

00:38:10.330 --> 00:38:13.090
And I didn't properly
engage those folks early on

00:38:13.090 --> 00:38:13.942
and I should of.

00:38:13.942 --> 00:38:15.400
Now, when I went
to directors and I

00:38:15.400 --> 00:38:17.108
said who can be Google
Guides, well, they

00:38:17.108 --> 00:38:18.837
gave the enthusiastic.

00:38:18.837 --> 00:38:21.170
They gave us the folks who
were anxious to move forward,

00:38:21.170 --> 00:38:26.110
which was helpful, except some
of the most impactful users

00:38:26.110 --> 00:38:29.860
that could change our
percentage chance for success

00:38:29.860 --> 00:38:34.112
were the folks who had such a
high stake in their knowledge

00:38:34.112 --> 00:38:36.070
of how they use the
existing tools because they

00:38:36.070 --> 00:38:40.120
had made themselves valuable
to their organization.

00:38:40.120 --> 00:38:41.680
And when we walked
in and said these

00:38:41.680 --> 00:38:43.660
are the tools
we're moving to, it

00:38:43.660 --> 00:38:46.040
was almost as if I had
taken something from them.

00:38:46.040 --> 00:38:48.610
It was almost as if
I was depriving them

00:38:48.610 --> 00:38:50.860
of that investment
in themselves.

00:38:50.860 --> 00:38:51.970
And I learned from that.

00:38:51.970 --> 00:38:53.440
It blew up because
these same folks

00:38:53.440 --> 00:38:54.850
could be the ones
that could find

00:38:54.850 --> 00:38:59.666
the most vehement reasons
why they couldn't use this,

00:38:59.666 --> 00:39:00.790
why they couldn't use that.

00:39:00.790 --> 00:39:02.230
It didn't matter what it was.

00:39:02.230 --> 00:39:03.580
It was, this is what
we know how to use

00:39:03.580 --> 00:39:05.020
and how are we
going to engage it.

00:39:05.020 --> 00:39:07.240
The other piece is I
failed to calculate

00:39:07.240 --> 00:39:11.560
how much the folks
around me, my IT staff,

00:39:11.560 --> 00:39:16.550
had a vested interest in
the existing solutions.

00:39:16.550 --> 00:39:22.390
So for an awful lot of them,
the status quo was validating.

00:39:22.390 --> 00:39:24.250
It was job security.

00:39:24.250 --> 00:39:31.480
It was almost a measure of
their worth to the organization

00:39:31.480 --> 00:39:33.820
and I needed to make sure
to engage effectively

00:39:33.820 --> 00:39:35.194
with them to win them over.

00:39:35.194 --> 00:39:36.610
And so, there were
a lot of things

00:39:36.610 --> 00:39:39.820
that we kind of shifted how
we went about this business,

00:39:39.820 --> 00:39:43.930
and I started going out and
meeting with the detractors,

00:39:43.930 --> 00:39:47.020
with the folks that were just
certain this couldn't work.

00:39:47.020 --> 00:39:48.910
We started running
grassroots trainings,

00:39:48.910 --> 00:39:50.740
and I would personally
go in and sit down

00:39:50.740 --> 00:39:53.170
with anyone in the agencies
that wished to attend,

00:39:53.170 --> 00:39:57.160
and we would talk about how they
had established and completed

00:39:57.160 --> 00:39:59.350
their workflows on the ground.

00:39:59.350 --> 00:40:02.000
And right up on the front
I would apologize to them

00:40:02.000 --> 00:40:04.840
for not properly
acknowledging what

00:40:04.840 --> 00:40:09.530
they had invested in themselves
to make the state successful.

00:40:09.530 --> 00:40:12.780
And in doing so, I was able
to get a fair number of them

00:40:12.780 --> 00:40:17.150
to kind of engage in
an acknowledgement

00:40:17.150 --> 00:40:20.600
that the potential existed with
these tools to scale to degrees

00:40:20.600 --> 00:40:22.550
they had never seen before.

00:40:22.550 --> 00:40:24.680
And I asked if they
would, please join me

00:40:24.680 --> 00:40:27.530
in researching if there
was potential in here

00:40:27.530 --> 00:40:29.960
to reinvent how state
government worked,

00:40:29.960 --> 00:40:31.880
and we dove in and
did that exactly.

00:40:31.880 --> 00:40:33.620
A couple of examples.

00:40:33.620 --> 00:40:37.460
I had this compressed
digital video box on my desk

00:40:37.460 --> 00:40:40.640
that was like $18,000,
and every cabinet

00:40:40.640 --> 00:40:43.550
had one for communicating
with the governor's office.

00:40:43.550 --> 00:40:46.280
It hadn't been on
in like three years.

00:40:46.280 --> 00:40:48.680
We were spending
over a million a year

00:40:48.680 --> 00:40:52.790
maintaining the bridges in
the systems for those devices.

00:40:52.790 --> 00:40:54.410
And by working with
Google, I actually

00:40:54.410 --> 00:40:56.090
got the opportunity
to go out and meet

00:40:56.090 --> 00:40:59.900
with some of the product
leads on pipeline

00:40:59.900 --> 00:41:01.970
and learn about what
was coming from Google.

00:41:01.970 --> 00:41:03.800
So before it was even
publicly announced,

00:41:03.800 --> 00:41:07.340
we were able to hear about
what they were doing with--

00:41:07.340 --> 00:41:09.440
at the time it was
called GVCs, and then it

00:41:09.440 --> 00:41:12.170
was Chromebox for Meetings,
and some of the capabilities

00:41:12.170 --> 00:41:14.940
that those devices brought
to the table for us.

00:41:14.940 --> 00:41:18.380
So we completely
reinvented how we engaged.

00:41:18.380 --> 00:41:20.330
The governor is an example.

00:41:20.330 --> 00:41:22.910
He wanted to go overseas
for Thanksgiving

00:41:22.910 --> 00:41:25.197
and provide dinner
for the troops.

00:41:25.197 --> 00:41:26.780
We had a lot of
National Guard members

00:41:26.780 --> 00:41:28.516
that were deployed overseas.

00:41:28.516 --> 00:41:30.140
He wanted to reach
out to me with them,

00:41:30.140 --> 00:41:32.570
but the formal budget press
conference for the governor's

00:41:32.570 --> 00:41:33.920
office was overlapped.

00:41:33.920 --> 00:41:36.470
It's scheduled at
the exact same time,

00:41:36.470 --> 00:41:40.040
and he had the idea to leverage
the technology as a force

00:41:40.040 --> 00:41:41.430
multiplier.

00:41:41.430 --> 00:41:42.920
So he went ahead,
he went overseas,

00:41:42.920 --> 00:41:45.590
he handled the dinners
with the troops,

00:41:45.590 --> 00:41:48.410
and he just stepped
into a little side room

00:41:48.410 --> 00:41:50.210
at an airport in
the Middle East.

00:41:50.210 --> 00:41:52.610
We put him in front of
a Nexus 7 and put him

00:41:52.610 --> 00:41:54.980
on the big screen in the
formal conference room.

00:41:54.980 --> 00:41:56.660
And he did his press conference.

00:41:56.660 --> 00:41:58.670
He looked in the
eye of the press.

00:41:58.670 --> 00:42:00.110
He greeted everyone.

00:42:00.110 --> 00:42:02.789
He handled the press
conference and the release.

00:42:02.789 --> 00:42:04.580
He even announced that
he was running again

00:42:04.580 --> 00:42:06.470
for the next term
as governor and then

00:42:06.470 --> 00:42:08.540
he went back to
doing what he did.

00:42:08.540 --> 00:42:10.490
And that type of
shift was something

00:42:10.490 --> 00:42:12.950
we never expected when
we adopted and moved

00:42:12.950 --> 00:42:15.440
towards G Suite.

00:42:15.440 --> 00:42:18.370
Another mistake that I think
I made is I thought of G Suite

00:42:18.370 --> 00:42:21.360
as a product line instead
of as an ecosystem,

00:42:21.360 --> 00:42:23.990
and there was so much
there that we could do.

00:42:23.990 --> 00:42:26.180
So much there that
we could build on.

00:42:26.180 --> 00:42:29.630
Before long, we stopped
developing thick client apps

00:42:29.630 --> 00:42:31.949
on the ground and
in our data centers.

00:42:31.949 --> 00:42:33.740
We started building
against Google's cloud.

00:42:33.740 --> 00:42:35.450
We started building
on App Engine,

00:42:35.450 --> 00:42:38.450
which allowed me to build
dynamic applications that

00:42:38.450 --> 00:42:41.791
continued to improve
as we used them,

00:42:41.791 --> 00:42:43.790
which was contrary to the
state's normal design.

00:42:43.790 --> 00:42:47.390
It had been before that where
you had a problem come forward,

00:42:47.390 --> 00:42:50.240
you went through the two
year process to do budget,

00:42:50.240 --> 00:42:52.070
you bought a physical
server, you threw it

00:42:52.070 --> 00:42:54.920
into a data center, and then you
started building the solution.

00:42:54.920 --> 00:42:56.840
And once you rolled it
out into deployment,

00:42:56.840 --> 00:43:00.220
it began to decay that day.

00:43:00.220 --> 00:43:02.710
By moving forward and
starting to build dynamically

00:43:02.710 --> 00:43:04.970
against the cloud
capabilities, we

00:43:04.970 --> 00:43:08.140
found that as Google improved
products, as improved security,

00:43:08.140 --> 00:43:10.480
as they continue to
evolve, our products built

00:43:10.480 --> 00:43:13.960
on the top of them,
also continue to rise.

00:43:13.960 --> 00:43:17.680
That's a big cultural shift
to get people used to.

00:43:17.680 --> 00:43:19.180
Those were things
that I kind of had

00:43:19.180 --> 00:43:22.870
to take on from a
people perspective

00:43:22.870 --> 00:43:26.500
to empower us to make the
most out of what we could do.

00:43:26.500 --> 00:43:29.890
But by the time we were done,
as I said, we consolidated IT.

00:43:29.890 --> 00:43:31.854
We closed our data centers.

00:43:31.854 --> 00:43:34.270
We started investing back in
those folks, those folks that

00:43:34.270 --> 00:43:36.250
were originally reluctant
because they thought

00:43:36.250 --> 00:43:37.755
you're taking their jobs away.

00:43:37.755 --> 00:43:39.130
Because if you
move to the cloud,

00:43:39.130 --> 00:43:41.050
why do they need someone
to take your cooling?

00:43:41.050 --> 00:43:44.810
And why do they need
someone to install servers?

00:43:44.810 --> 00:43:47.660
We were able to engage with
them, manage the communication,

00:43:47.660 --> 00:43:51.530
and convince them that we were
going to invest back in them.

00:43:51.530 --> 00:43:53.750
And so I started doing
cybersecurity initiatives

00:43:53.750 --> 00:43:55.780
and training folks
on cybersecurity.

00:43:55.780 --> 00:43:59.210
I started raising the skill
sets so that they could manage

00:43:59.210 --> 00:44:02.960
far more deployments because
I had the cloud capability,

00:44:02.960 --> 00:44:07.110
and I got them out of the
power and cooling business.

00:44:07.110 --> 00:44:11.850
Eventually, they came to realize
that their resume was so much

00:44:11.850 --> 00:44:16.450
more impactful because of what
they could do very quickly

00:44:16.450 --> 00:44:18.070
and how they could
deliver for us.

00:44:18.070 --> 00:44:18.820
It was a big deal.

00:44:18.820 --> 00:44:21.100
So much so, that the
governor's efforts

00:44:21.100 --> 00:44:25.495
to deliver IT as a
quality of life issue

00:44:25.495 --> 00:44:26.830
were highly successful.

00:44:26.830 --> 00:44:29.140
We deployed 10,000 users
across our enterprise,

00:44:29.140 --> 00:44:30.490
all of the executive branch.

00:44:30.490 --> 00:44:32.200
We did 100%.

00:44:32.200 --> 00:44:34.330
We actually achieved
an environment where

00:44:34.330 --> 00:44:36.760
we had security protection
and certifications on the data

00:44:36.760 --> 00:44:38.290
centers that our data went into.

00:44:38.290 --> 00:44:40.460
We hadn't before that.

00:44:40.460 --> 00:44:44.330
We deployed 100,000 user
license for all students

00:44:44.330 --> 00:44:46.100
in the State of Wyoming.

00:44:46.100 --> 00:44:49.520
We rolled that out so that if
you were involved in education,

00:44:49.520 --> 00:44:52.220
your district could get free
licenses for all the folks, all

00:44:52.220 --> 00:44:56.540
the students, the parents,
the alumni, the teachers.

00:44:56.540 --> 00:44:59.210
We rolled all that out as well.

00:44:59.210 --> 00:45:01.940
So much so, that even the
governor's state of the state

00:45:01.940 --> 00:45:05.690
address was led out of the
chamber between the Senate

00:45:05.690 --> 00:45:09.250
and the House as a
YouTube live event.

00:45:09.250 --> 00:45:11.170
When he did his
energy policy release,

00:45:11.170 --> 00:45:13.420
that was a Hangout
on air at the time,

00:45:13.420 --> 00:45:15.400
where people could
actually type in questions.

00:45:15.400 --> 00:45:19.140
Citizens could engage
in typing questions.

00:45:19.140 --> 00:45:22.860
We closed about half of our
meeting rooms in our buildings

00:45:22.860 --> 00:45:24.340
and we moved to Scrums.

00:45:24.340 --> 00:45:26.610
Because if you give
a government employee

00:45:26.610 --> 00:45:28.620
an hour of schedule
in a meeting room,

00:45:28.620 --> 00:45:30.620
it doesn't matter if
you're done for 10 minutes.

00:45:30.620 --> 00:45:32.550
They will sit-in
that room for an hour

00:45:32.550 --> 00:45:34.560
because they don't
have go back yet.

00:45:34.560 --> 00:45:35.340
We changed that.

00:45:35.340 --> 00:45:36.840
We kind of changed
the expectations.

00:45:36.840 --> 00:45:39.700
We allowed them to
work from anywhere.

00:45:39.700 --> 00:45:44.020
We put the Chromebox for
meetings and Chromecasts

00:45:44.020 --> 00:45:48.101
in all the hallways and started
doing standup meetings, Scrums.

00:45:48.101 --> 00:45:50.350
And normally a Scrum you'd
use in an agile development

00:45:50.350 --> 00:45:51.140
environment.

00:45:51.140 --> 00:45:54.110
We started doing Scrums for
business management as well.

00:45:54.110 --> 00:45:58.300
So we started doing them
just for managing team leads.

00:45:58.300 --> 00:46:00.340
We were in an old
junior high school.

00:46:00.340 --> 00:46:02.290
It had great big
wide hallways, so we

00:46:02.290 --> 00:46:05.550
put in these high
standing tables, and all

00:46:05.550 --> 00:46:07.690
the white boards
and the displays up,

00:46:07.690 --> 00:46:10.276
and then you could just
walk through a meeting.

00:46:10.276 --> 00:46:12.400
So the network team would
be designing what we were

00:46:12.400 --> 00:46:14.455
building our for our backbone.

00:46:14.455 --> 00:46:17.080
You could stop in on the server
team and hear what was going on

00:46:17.080 --> 00:46:18.760
and give input.

00:46:18.760 --> 00:46:20.830
The day I started, the
President of the Senate

00:46:20.830 --> 00:46:23.110
was champion building
a $40 billion

00:46:23.110 --> 00:46:24.830
data center for the
State of Wyoming.

00:46:27.430 --> 00:46:30.640
The President of the Senate,
well, he's a very powerful man

00:46:30.640 --> 00:46:34.695
and I had the privilege
to go in front of him

00:46:34.695 --> 00:46:36.974
and say with appropriate
respect, Mr President,

00:46:36.974 --> 00:46:38.640
I think that might
not be the best idea.

00:46:38.640 --> 00:46:39.510
It will be great.

00:46:39.510 --> 00:46:41.580
I'll have this
beautiful corner office

00:46:41.580 --> 00:46:44.340
in this massive single
point of failure,

00:46:44.340 --> 00:46:48.520
and I'm not sure that's
good for us in the long run.

00:46:48.520 --> 00:46:51.880
I got a smart bump for
that one, by the way.

00:46:51.880 --> 00:46:54.100
When it was done, instead
we invested in the backbone

00:46:54.100 --> 00:46:57.910
and we rolled out an IP
Version 6, 100 gig backbone

00:46:57.910 --> 00:47:02.190
throughout all of Wyoming
and closed our data centers.

00:47:02.190 --> 00:47:07.410
And we moved so that the
student, the professor,

00:47:07.410 --> 00:47:10.200
the teacher, the state
employee, whoever

00:47:10.200 --> 00:47:12.150
had the next brilliant
idea, I didn't

00:47:12.150 --> 00:47:13.865
have to know who they
were going to be.

00:47:13.865 --> 00:47:15.240
I just had to know
that they were

00:47:15.240 --> 00:47:17.698
going to be sitting in a point
where they had the capacity,

00:47:17.698 --> 00:47:19.620
they had the bandwidth
to deliver it,

00:47:19.620 --> 00:47:21.990
and they could build it
directly to the cloud,

00:47:21.990 --> 00:47:23.445
and that was huge for us.

00:47:23.445 --> 00:47:25.320
Now, there's a whole
bunch more that happened

00:47:25.320 --> 00:47:26.403
during the course of that.

00:47:26.403 --> 00:47:28.400
One of my data centers blew up.

00:47:28.400 --> 00:47:32.210
I had another one that they tore
the walls down on the side of.

00:47:32.210 --> 00:47:35.490
I have a four o'clock session,
so here's my shameless plug.

00:47:35.490 --> 00:47:38.220
I will tell the whole
Wyoming story during that,

00:47:38.220 --> 00:47:41.250
but I ended up being such a
believer in what it empowered

00:47:41.250 --> 00:47:45.960
for governor of Wyoming,
that after my term,

00:47:45.960 --> 00:47:49.020
I asked for his permission
to pursue Google.

00:47:49.020 --> 00:47:52.020
And so a little over a year
ago he gave me permission.

00:47:52.020 --> 00:47:55.170
We separated the authorities so
that I was no longer reviewing

00:47:55.170 --> 00:47:56.850
anything related
to Google, and I

00:47:56.850 --> 00:47:59.946
started pursuing a position with
Google because I'm a disruptor.

00:47:59.946 --> 00:48:01.320
I'm one of those
folks that likes

00:48:01.320 --> 00:48:03.690
to go in and break things,
and it got to the point

00:48:03.690 --> 00:48:07.590
that we had evolved so much in
Wyoming that my deputy CIO was

00:48:07.590 --> 00:48:09.930
spending a lot of her
time just keeping me

00:48:09.930 --> 00:48:12.750
from wandering the halls,
terrorizing the villagers.

00:48:12.750 --> 00:48:15.310
And I knew it was time to
go on and do something else,

00:48:15.310 --> 00:48:17.584
and so I pursued Google
so that I could come here,

00:48:17.584 --> 00:48:19.250
and I now work in
professional services.

00:48:19.250 --> 00:48:20.692
I'm not in sales.

00:48:20.692 --> 00:48:21.900
I'm in professional services.

00:48:21.900 --> 00:48:26.731
So I help governments impact
quality of life for citizens

00:48:26.731 --> 00:48:28.230
and it's been a
huge success for us,

00:48:28.230 --> 00:48:30.330
but there were a lot of
things I didn't anticipate,

00:48:30.330 --> 00:48:31.913
and across the board
they were related

00:48:31.913 --> 00:48:36.180
to people, not technology.

00:48:36.180 --> 00:48:37.430
as

